Title: The Velveteen Rabbit

Credit: Written by

Author: Margery Williams

Source: Adapted from book

Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. NURSERY - CHRISTMAS MORNING

A charming VELVETEEN RABBIT sits atop a stocking, nestled among nuts, oranges, and a toy engine. His coat is spotted brown and white, with pink sateen-lined ears.

NARRATOR

There was once a velveteen rabbit, splendid and new. On Christmas morning, he sat in the Boy's stocking, the best of all gifts.

The BOY, eyes wide with excitement, pulls the Rabbit from the stocking.

NARRATOR

For two hours, the Boy loved him. But soon, the Rabbit was forgotten amidst the excitement of new presents.

INT. NURSERY - DAY

The Velveteen Rabbit lies on the nursery floor, surrounded by superior mechanical toys. The SKIN HORSE, old and wise, sits nearby.

VELVETEEN RABBIT

What is REAL?

SKIN HORSE

Real isn't how you're made. It's a thing that happens when a child loves you for a long time.

VELVETEEN RABBIT

Does it hurt?

SKIN HORSE

Sometimes. But when you're Real, you don't mind being hurt.

The Rabbit listens intently, longing to become Real.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

NANA, bustling about, searches for a toy. She spots the Velveteen Rabbit.

NANA

Here, take your old Bunny!

She hands the Rabbit to the Boy, who hugs him tightly.

NARRATOR

That night, and many nights after, the Rabbit slept in the Boy's bed, feeling the warmth of love.

EXT. GARDEN - SPRING DAY

The Boy plays in the garden, the Velveteen Rabbit by his side. They enjoy picnics and fairy huts under raspberry canes.

NARRATOR

Spring came, and the Rabbit was happy, never noticing how his fur grew shabbier.

The Boy hugs the Rabbit tightly, rubbing off the pink from his nose.

EXT. WOOD - SUMMER EVENING

The Rabbit lies in the bracken, watching ants. Two wild RABBITS approach, curious.

WILD RABBIT

Why don't you play with us?

VELVETEEN RABBIT

I don't feel like it.

The wild rabbits laugh, noticing his lack of hind legs.

WILD RABBIT

He isn't real!

VELVETEEN RABBIT

I am Real! The Boy said so!

The Boy's footsteps scare the wild rabbits away.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

The Boy lies ill in bed, the Velveteen Rabbit hidden under the covers.

NARRATOR

The Boy was ill, and the Rabbit stayed close, whispering dreams of better days.

The Boy recovers, sitting up with the Rabbit by his side.

EXT. GARDEN - DAY

The Rabbit, now old and shabby, is placed in a sack with old toys, destined for a bonfire.

NARRATOR

The Boy was going to the seaside, but the Rabbit was to be burnt.

A tear falls from the Rabbit's eye, landing on the ground.

EXT. GARDEN - NIGHT

A magical flower blooms where the tear fell. A FAIRY emerges, radiant and kind.

FAIRY

Little Rabbit, don't you know who I am?

VELVETEEN RABBIT

Are you the nursery magic Fairy?

FAIRY

Yes. I take care of loved playthings and make them Real.

She kisses the Rabbit, transforming him.

EXT. WOOD - NIGHT

The Velveteen Rabbit, now a Real Rabbit, joins the wild rabbits, leaping with joy.

NARRATOR

He was a Real Rabbit at last, at home with the others.

EXT. WOOD - SPRING DAY

The Boy plays in the wood, spotting two rabbits. One has familiar markings.

BOY

Why, he looks just like my old Bunny!

The Rabbit watches the Boy, grateful for the love that made him Real.

FADE OUT.

THE END.
